---
import TextPage from '../layouts/TextPage.astro';

const description =
  'Get help with Gleamit, move your data to a new device, and delete the optional backup from your own Google Drive or iCloud.';

const sections = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'contact', text: 'Contact' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'new-device', text: 'Restore data on a new device' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'delete-backup', text: 'Delete the cloud backup' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'delete-all', text: 'Delete all Gleamit data' },
];
---

<TextPage title="Support" description={description} eyebrow="Help" sections={sections}>
  <p class="lead" slot="masthead">
    Gleamit keeps your data on your device. This page explains how to take it to a new device, and how to remove the
    optional cloud backup yourself.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
  <div class="card contact" id="contact-card">
    <div>
      <strong>support@gleamit.app</strong>
      <p class="muted">Questions, bug reports, purchase problems. You get a reply within 3 business days.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="btn" href="mailto:support@gleamit.app">Email support</a>
  </div>

  <h2 id="new-device">Restore data on a new device</h2>
  <p>
    There is no Gleamit account, so nothing moves to a new device by itself. Your data travels in one of two ways: the
    cloud backup, which stays on the same platform, or a backup file you export yourself, which works between any two
    devices.
  </p>
  <p class="card note" id="restore-adds">
    <strong>Restoring adds the backup to what is already on the device.</strong> Nothing is replaced or deleted, so it
    is safe to restore after you have started using Gleamit on the new device.
  </p>

  <h3 id="ios-to-ios">iOS to iOS</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        On the old iPhone or iPad, open Gleamit, go to <kbd>Settings</kbd>, then <kbd>Data Backup</kbd>, and check that <kbd>Cloud backup</kbd> is on. Tap <kbd>Back up now</kbd>.
      </li>
      <li>Sign in to the new iPhone or iPad with the same Apple Account, with iCloud Drive on.</li>
      <li>Install Gleamit and open it. When it offers cloud backup, tap <kbd>I already have a backup</kbd>.</li>
      <li>Check the date and size of the backup it found, then tap <kbd>Restore</kbd>.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">
    The restore option only shows up once iCloud has brought the backup to the new device, which can take a few
    minutes. If you missed it, <kbd>Settings</kbd>, then <kbd>Data Backup</kbd>, then <kbd>Restore from cloud backup</kbd> does the same at any time.
  </p>

  <h3 id="android-to-android">Android to Android</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        On the old device, open Gleamit, go to <kbd>Settings</kbd>, then <kbd>Data Backup</kbd>, and check that <kbd>Cloud backup</kbd> is on. Tap <kbd>Back up now</kbd>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Install Gleamit on the new device and open it. When it offers cloud backup, tap <kbd>I already have a backup</kbd>.
      </li>
      <li>Pick the same Google account the old device backed up to.</li>
      <li>Check the date and size of the backup it found, then tap <kbd>Restore</kbd>.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">
    If you missed it, <kbd>Settings</kbd>, then <kbd>Data Backup</kbd>, then <kbd>Restore from cloud backup</kbd> does
    the same at any time.
  </p>

  <h3 id="cross-platform">Between iOS and Android</h3>
  <p>
    The cloud backup lives in iCloud on iOS and in Google Drive on Android, so it cannot cross over. Move a backup
    file instead.
  </p>
  <div class="card">
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        On the old device, open Gleamit, go to <kbd>Settings</kbd>, then <kbd>Data Backup</kbd>, then <kbd>Manual backup</kbd>, and tap <kbd>Export full backup</kbd>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Save or send the <kbd>gleamit-backup.zip</kbd> file somewhere the new device can reach: an email to yourself, or
        a cloud storage app you use on both devices.
      </li>
      <li>Download the file on the new device.</li>
      <li>
        Install Gleamit on the new device, go to <kbd>Settings</kbd>, then <kbd>Data Backup</kbd>, then <kbd>Manual backup</kbd>, and tap <kbd>Import full backup</kbd>. Pick the file.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">The same file also works between two devices on the same platform, with or without cloud backup.</p>

  <h3 id="new-device-pro">Your Pro purchase</h3>
  <p id="pro-restore">
    Pro belongs to your App Store or Google Play account, not to the app data, so a backup does not carry it. On a new
    device of the same platform, signed in to the same store account, tap <kbd>Restore purchase</kbd>. A purchase made
    on one store does not carry over to the other: the App Store and Google Play keep separate purchases.
  </p>

  <h2 id="delete-backup">Delete the cloud backup</h2>
  <p>
    Turning cloud backup off in Gleamit only stops new uploads. The copy already in your cloud stays there until you
    delete it yourself, following the steps for your platform.
  </p>

  <h3 id="android"><span class="platform">Android</span></h3>
  <p>
    When cloud backup is on, Gleamit uploads a copy of your data to a private app area of the Google Drive account you
    picked. Only Gleamit can see that area, and it cannot see anything else in your Drive.
  </p>
  <p class="card note" id="android-switch">
    <strong>Turning backup off in Gleamit, or disconnecting the Google account in Gleamit, does not delete the copy
    already in Drive.</strong> It only stops new uploads. The copy stays in your Drive until you delete it there.
  </p>
  <div class="card" id="android-delete">
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Open the <kbd>Google Drive</kbd> app, or go to <kbd>drive.google.com</kbd> in a browser.</li>
      <li>Tap the menu (three lines) or your profile picture, then <kbd>Settings</kbd>.</li>
      <li>Choose <kbd>Manage apps</kbd>.</li>
      <li>Find <kbd>Gleamit</kbd> in the list.</li>
      <li>Tap <kbd>Options</kbd> next to it, then <kbd>Delete hidden app data</kbd>. Confirm.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">
    <kbd>Disconnect from Drive</kbd>, in the same menu, is the other way round: it takes Gleamit's access away and deletes
    the copy with it.
  </p>
  <p class="muted">If you connected more than one Google account over time, repeat these steps in each account.</p>

  <h3 id="ios"><span class="platform">iOS</span></h3>
  <p>
    When cloud backup is on, Gleamit writes a copy of your data into a private area of your iCloud Drive, hidden from the
    Files app so it cannot be moved or deleted by accident. iCloud does the uploading, following your iCloud settings.
  </p>
  <p class="card note" id="ios-switch">
    <strong>Turning backup off does not delete the copy already in iCloud.</strong> It only stops new uploads. The copy
    stays in your iCloud storage until you delete it there.
  </p>
  <p>On your iPhone or iPad:</p>
  <div class="card" id="ios-delete">
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Open <kbd>Settings</kbd> on your iPhone or iPad.</li>
      <li>Tap your name at the top to open <kbd>Apple Account</kbd>, then <kbd>iCloud</kbd>.</li>
      <li>Tap <kbd>Manage Account Storage</kbd>. On older iOS versions this is <kbd>Manage Storage</kbd>.</li>
      <li>Find <kbd>Gleamit</kbd> in the list of apps using iCloud storage.</li>
      <li>Tap it, then <kbd>Delete Data from iCloud</kbd>. Confirm.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
  <p>Or on a Mac:</p>
  <div class="card" id="ios-delete-mac">
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Open <kbd>System Settings</kbd>, then <kbd>Apple Account</kbd> at the top.</li>
      <li>Click <kbd>iCloud</kbd>, then <kbd>Manage</kbd>.</li>
      <li>Find <kbd>Gleamit</kbd> in the list.</li>
      <li>Click it, then <kbd>Delete Documents and Data</kbd>. Confirm.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <h2 id="delete-all">Delete all Gleamit data</h2>
  <p>
    Deleting the app removes everything stored on the device: brushing sessions, teeth, photos, documents, reminders and
    settings. There is no Gleamit account and no Gleamit server, so none of what you record ever reaches us.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your own cloud storage is the one place a copy of that data outlives the app. If cloud backup was ever
    on, <a href="#delete-backup">delete the cloud backup</a> as well, so nothing is left in your Google Drive or your
    iCloud.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your Pro purchase is tied to your App Store or Google Play account, not to the app data. Reinstall Gleamit and tap <kbd>Restore purchase</kbd> to get it back.
  </p>
  <p id="purchase-record">
    If you bought Pro, RevenueCat keeps a purchase record for us, and uninstalling does not delete it. To have it
    deleted, email <a href="mailto:support@gleamit.app">support@gleamit.app</a> with the order number from your store
    receipt. The purchase itself stays with your store account. If you later tap <kbd>Restore purchase</kbd>, Pro
    comes back, and so does a purchase record at RevenueCat.
  </p>
</TextPage>
